# In-container sshd, baked into gsrpi-dev-base and shared by every container built on
# it. Runs as the unprivileged `dev` user on port 2222, which each repo's compose
# publishes on a loopback port of its host — 2225 for infra-dev, 2224 for or3-dev.
#
# What it is FOR differs between those two, and the difference is the whole point of
# each. or3-dev's sshd is also a tunnel endpoint — the phone terminates a reverse
# forward on it to reach the vessel LAN. infra-dev's is a DOOR AND NOTHING ELSE: you
# ssh in from the phone or a PC and work in an abduco session, and the model traffic
# that used to cross the phone's metered radio goes out over zero's home connection
# instead. Nothing tunnels back through infra-dev.
#
# THE DEFAULTS HERE ARE THE SMALLER THING, and a child widens them in its own file.
# sshd takes the FIRST value it obtains for each keyword, so the Include below — at the
# top, before anything else is set — is what lets a child override rather than merely
# append. `AllowTcpForwarding yes` in a child's drop-in wins; the same line at the
# bottom of this file would not have.
#
# A glob that matches nothing is not an error, so a container with no drop-in gets
# exactly this file. The directory is created in the base image so that stays true.
#
# A `Match` block inside a drop-in is CONFINED TO THAT FILE and does not leak back into
# this one — measured on OpenSSH 9.2, not inferred: a drop-in ending in a `Match`
# leaves `Port` and `HostKey` below valid here, and `sshd -t` accepts the pair. (The
# same two lines *after* a `Match` in a single file are rejected outright with
# "Directive 'Port' is not allowed within a Match block", which is the behaviour that
# makes the leak sound plausible.) So a drop-in cannot break this file by structure;
# it breaks it by being wrong on its own terms, which `sshd -t` catches in entrypoint.sh
# before anything is exec'd.
Include /home/dev/sshd_config.d/*.conf

Port 2222
ListenAddress 0.0.0.0

# Persisted in the infra-ssh-host volume so a client's known_hosts entry survives a
# rebuild. Regenerating it every build would train you to accept a changed host key,
# which is the one habit that makes the check worthless.
HostKey /home/dev/.ssh-host/ssh_host_ed25519_key
PidFile /home/dev/.ssh-host/sshd.pid

# Key-only, `dev` only. A non-root sshd can only ever serve its own account.
AuthorizedKeysFile /home/dev/.ssh/authorized_keys
PubkeyAuthentication yes
PasswordAuthentication no
KbdInteractiveAuthentication no
PermitRootLogin no
UsePAM no                 # PAM needs root; a non-root sshd must disable it

# NO forwarding, as the base default. or3-dev turns it back on in
# sshd_config.d/10-forwarding.conf because a reverse forward is its reason to exist;
# nothing infra-dev does needs one, and this container holds ssh keys that reach all
# three hosts as an account in `wheel`. Off is the smaller thing to defend, and turning
# it on should be a deliberate drop-in with a reason written next to it.
AllowTcpForwarding no
GatewayPorts no

# Let ssh sessions inherit the env the entrypoint writes to ~/.ssh/environment —
# ANSIBLE_CONFIG among it, which is what makes `ansible fleet -m ping` work from any
# directory in an ssh session rather than only from /workspace/ansible.
PermitUserEnvironment yes

# Compression, stated rather than inherited. `sshd -T` already reports `compression yes`
# on OpenSSH 9.2 — this changes no behaviour and is here because the behaviour is worth
# being able to see. It is also only half the switch: compression is negotiated, the ssh
# CLIENT defaults to `no`, and both ends have to offer it. The half that turns it on is
# in ansible/playbooks/dev-client.yml, on the aliases the phone uses.
#
# It is worth having on THIS session, which is not true of every sshd. or3's ssh_config
# has the argument: compression belongs on the session whose plaintext it is, because it
# happens before encryption, and ciphertext does not compress. Here the phone's ssh client
# terminates on this sshd — cloudflared is transport underneath, not another ssh session —
# so this IS that session, and what it compresses is claude's output on its way to a
# metered radio. or3's own sshd is the other case: it carries an inner session that is
# already ciphertext, and setting it there would buy nothing.
Compression yes

# Drop a wedged session rather than leaving one that accepts input and never answers:
# 3 x 30s of silence and it is gone. abduco is what makes that free — the work is not
# in the ssh session, so losing the session loses nothing.
ClientAliveInterval 30
ClientAliveCountMax 3

Subsystem sftp internal-sftp
StrictModes yes
